7] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493849 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "97f16011-48a5-5603-ae76-0cd52cee0497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493901 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $13$ and $8,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493911 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $13,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=13\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{13}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493930 #edb/ref 1071 "A $